+
+Three consecutive tower-vantage reads across 2m19s, all identical — persistent, not a transient blip. Board shape at read time: 18 healthy / 2 degraded (render duo, unchanged #2225 residual) / **1 unreachable (telegram-primary)**.
+
+## Analysis
+
+**Healthy window between outages: bounded [18:44Z, 21:05:39Z] — at most 2h22m; tighter bound [19:07:21Z, 21:05:39Z] ≤1h58m** because 19:07:21Z is the last positive confirm before the gap. Two outage windows inside ~4 hours (17:21Z and ≤21:05Z) with byte-identical failure signatures (null HTTP status, `fetch failed`) establish a **pattern, not an incident**: intermittent tower/Render egress-path instability toward api.telegram.org.
+
+**On the redeploy-correlation hypothesis (from the first receipt):** the correlation claim was honest ("correlated, not proven") and this recurrence is the discriminating evidence arriving early. Two readings survive: (a) the 18:39:16Z redeploy genuinely cleared stale in-container state (DNS/egress) which then re-accumulated over ~2-3h — consistent with the #2212 restart precedent class; (b) the original correlation was coincidental and the underlying instability self-heals and re-fires on its own schedule, with the redeploy merely adjacent. Window 1's 3h healthy period immediately after the redeploy weakens pure-coincidence (b) but cannot eliminate it. **The discriminating experiment is exactly the one this receipt cannot perform**: in-container verbose fetch + DNS resolution during a live DOWN window (NOW — window 2 is open), compared against the same probe from a Render job or the dashboard console.
+
+**Alert-plane exposure arithmetic (updated):** two blind windows in ~4h; window 1 ≥47min (bounded ≤1h23m); window 2 open at filing time. During both, every tower-side alert surface (`notify_send_telegram`, `redis_quota_alert`, incident notifications) silently drops. If a monitor-plane SLA is ever formalized (first receipt §3), the datum is now: 2 incidents / 4h, MTTR-1 ≈ ≤1h23m, MTTR-2 open — an availability profile materially worse than "one stale-DNS blip".
+
+## What this changes for the admin
+
+1. **The diagnostic directive is now live, not hypothetical**: window 2 is open at filing time. In-container diagnostics captured BEFORE any restart/redeploy would discriminate hypothesis (a) from (b) in one shot — the exact evidence class the tower MCP plane cannot reach (first receipt §7: no exec surface, deploy events API unreachable).
+2. **A restart is no longer presumed sufficient.** Window 1 recovered adjacent to a redeploy; if window 2 is cleared the same way and recurs a third time, the platform-plane lead (Render region egress / upstream route instability, per the original filing's escalation row) becomes primary and Render support should be engaged with this timeline.
+3. **A gap remains structural**: no board tool probes the notify plane between sentry generations. A one-line addition of telegram-primary to the sentry provider set (or any existing recurring sweep) would have caught window 2's onset within minutes instead of leaving a 2-hour observation gap — smallest possible monitoring fix, admin/policy-lane, noted here without filing a separate finding (zero-duplicate).
+
+## Rollback path
+
+Revert-safe: one additive report doc under the reports directory; delete the single file to revert. No code, no protected paths, no workflow surface, no dependency added, nothing auto-runs the file. Zero mutations performed on any tower, Render, or Telegram surface during evidence collection.
